TEST_0011_cube_pml_extrude
==========================

Demonstrates PML added to an *imported* mesh by extrusion, via the optional
pml.input file read by the 3D mesher. This is the general-mesh counterpart of
the "on the fly" PML (mat.dat), which only works for the structured grid.

Pipeline
--------
1. gen_cube_h5.py writes cube.h5: a bare [0,300]^3 cube, 6^3 Hexa8, one solid
   material, NO PML.
2. The mesher (menu option 4, HDF5) reads cube.h5 and material.input, then sees
   pml.input and extrudes PML element layers on the requested sides, deriving
   the PML materials, rewriting material.input and appending PML blocks to
   material.spec.
3. sem3d runs the resulting mesh4spec.

Files
-----
  gen_cube_h5.py   mesh generator (bare cube, no PML)
  mesh.input       stdin for the mesher (1 proc, option 4, cube.h5)
  pml.input        PML sides + thickness: x-/x+/y-/y+ = 3 layers, z- = 3 layers,
                   z+ left free (free surface)
  material.input   base materials in (1 solid); rewritten by the mesher with the
                   derived PML materials appended
  material.spec    optional; the mesher appends `copy`+`domain=solidpml` blocks
  input.spec       sem3d config (ricker pulse at the cube centre 150,150,150)
  stations.txt     two receivers inside the physical domain
  run.sh           driver: gen mesh -> mesher -> (optional) sem3d

Run
---
  MESHER=/path/to/build/MESH/mesher ./run.sh
  # add SEM3D=/path/to/build/SEM3D/sem3d to also run the solver

Expected mesher result
----------------------
Bare cube = 216 solid elements. After extrusion (3 layers on 5 sides, corners
and edges combined) the mesh has 5 active sides, so every PML side-combination
that occurs on those 5 faces is created as a distinct PML material; the physical
(non-PML) element count stays 216. Waves from the central pulse should leave the
domain through the five PML faces without visible reflection; the z+ face is a
true free surface.

Reference: compare against TEST_0001_cube_pulse_ricker, whose PML is instead
built on the fly from mat.dat over the same geometry.
